module std.ffi
//! The Lyra half of the C boundary.
//!
//! An `extern` declares what C provides and the compiler checks the call; what it cannot
//! do is make the result *safe*, because C hands back a pointer and nothing else. A raw
//! pointer carries no length, so nothing about it can be checked — `p.offset(n)^` will
//! read whatever is at that address, and `unsafe` is the only thing standing between a
//! program and that fact.
//!
//! **A pointer and a length together can be checked**, and that is the whole idea here:
//! `CBuffer` pairs the two, `get` traps on an index outside them exactly as `xs[i]` does,
//! and `unsafe` appears once — in this file — instead of at every use. It is the same
//! division `random_seed`/`Rng` and `read_line`/`parse_i64` already draw: the compiler
//! provides only what cannot be written in Lyra, and the part that can be is written here.
//!
//! What it does **not** do is make the pointer valid. Nothing can: a `CBuffer` over
//! memory C has freed, or built with a length larger than the allocation, checks against
//! the wrong number and reads out of bounds without complaint. Constructing one is the
//! promise; the checking is what the promise then buys.
//!
//! ```
//! import std.ffi.{ CBuffer, cstring_len }
//!
//! let read_c_string = (p: ^u8) -> CBuffer =>
//!   unsafe { CBuffer { ptr: p, len: cstring_len(p) } }
//! ```
//!
//! A buffer Lyra owns works too — `CBuffer { ptr: unsafe { &mut xs[0] }, len: xs.len() }`
//! — since a `^mut T` is assignable to a `^T`. Reading one that way buys nothing `xs[i]`
//! does not already give you; it is for handing the same bytes to C and back.

/// A foreign buffer: a pointer, and the number of bytes it is valid for.
///
/// Bytes rather than a generic element type, because that is what crosses the boundary —
/// `^u8` is what `lyra-E063` leaves you holding for a string, an array or any other block
/// of memory, and a typed view over it would be a second question (alignment) on top of
/// this one.
///
/// The fields are public because building one *is* the unsafe act, and hiding them behind
/// a constructor would only add a place to look: a caller who can produce the pointer can
/// already produce a wrong length.
pub struct CBuffer {
  /// Where the bytes are. Not owned — this type never frees anything, on the standing
  /// rule that ownership does not cross the boundary in either direction.
  ptr: ^u8,
  /// How many bytes `ptr` is valid for. Whoever built the buffer promised this.
  len: i64,
}

/// The byte at `i`.
///
/// `pure` here means what the compiler means by it — no tracked effect — and **not** that
/// two calls must agree. The bytes belong to someone else, who may write them between one
/// call and the next; that is a property of foreign memory rather than of this function,
/// and no bound could describe it.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `i` is outside the buffer, which is the same answer `xs[i]` gives and the
/// reason this type exists. The message is a constant rather than an interpolation, so
/// `get` allocates nothing and stays callable from `noalloc` code.
pub let get = pure noalloc (self: CBuffer, i: i64) -> u8 => {
  if i < 0 || i >= self.len {
    panic("std.ffi: CBuffer index out of bounds")
  }
  unsafe { self.ptr.offset(i)^ }
}

/// The length of a NUL-terminated C string, not counting the NUL — `strlen`, in Lyra.
///
/// **Written here rather than declared as an `extern`**, which is the division the whole
/// language follows: anything expressible in Lyra goes in the library, and only what
/// genuinely cannot be stays primitive (`random_seed` is a builtin, `Rng` is not;
/// `read_line` is, `parse_i64` is not). Scanning for a zero byte became expressible the
/// moment `p.offset(n)` existed, and writing it here settles two questions rather than
/// moving them: whether C's return is `u64` or something else on this target, and which
/// library a `@link` would have to name.
///
/// The cost is that libc's `strlen` is hand-vectorised and this is a byte at a time. The
/// prelude's `index` is a naive scan on the same reasoning — a real guarantee would want
/// a `memmem`-shaped builtin, and until there is one, honest and slow beats an ABI claim
/// nobody checks.
///
/// **`unsafe`, because the terminator is the caller's promise.** `CBuffer.get` needs no
/// such marking: its length was promised once, at construction, and every read after that
/// is checked against it. This *is* that promise — "there is a zero byte somewhere ahead"
/// — and nothing here can verify it. A pointer into memory with no NUL runs off the end
/// exactly as `strlen` does.
pub let cstring_len = unsafe pure noalloc (p: ^u8) -> i64 => {
  var n = 0
  var b = unsafe { p^ }
  for b != 0 {
    n += 1
    b = unsafe { p.offset(n)^ }
  }
  n
}

/// The buffer's bytes as a Lyra string, read as UTF-8.
///
/// The same name the byte-array builtin has, because it is the same operation on the same
/// bytes — only the container differs, and a reader should not have to remember two words
/// for it. The receiver tells them apart.
///
/// **It copies, twice**, and neither copy is avoidable. A string is a ref-counted box whose
/// header sits at its start, so it cannot point into memory it does not own — foreign
/// memory least of all, which may be freed or rewritten the moment the call returns. The
/// bytes are gathered into a `[]u8` and then decoded into a fresh string; each pass is
/// O(n), and the array's growth is amortized doubling.
///
/// Invalid UTF-8 is not rejected, matching `read_line` and the builtin: the rune count is
/// the number of non-continuation bytes, so malformed input yields a string whose length
/// disagrees with what it holds. One unvalidated answer is better than two different ones.
pub let decode_utf8 = pure (self: CBuffer) -> string => {
  var bytes: []u8 = []
  for i in 0..<self.len { bytes.push(self.get(i)) }
  bytes.decode_utf8()
}

/// A NUL-terminated copy of `self`, to hand to a C function taking `char*`.
///
/// ```
/// import std.ffi.{ cstring }
///
/// var buf = path.cstring()
/// let fd = unsafe { open(&buf[0], 0) }
/// ```
///
/// **A plain `[]u8` rather than a `CString` type**, which is a decision worth stating
/// because every language with an FFI makes it differently. Three things settled it here:
///
/// The dangerous shape is already refused. `open(&"x".cstring()[0], 0)` — the pointer into
/// a buffer that stops existing at the end of the statement — is `lyra-E059`, *cannot take
/// the address of a temporary*. That is the exact bug Rust's `CString::new(…).as_ptr()`
/// produces silently and that Clippy carries a dedicated lint for, and Lyra's type checker
/// refuses it outright. A wrapper type would not add to that.
///
/// A struct pairing the bytes with a stored pointer is *worse*, not better. A `[]u8`'s
/// elements live behind a pointer in the box, so growth reallocates them: a pointer stored
/// at construction goes stale on the next `push` while the struct holding it is perfectly
/// alive, and the read is silent garbage. Recomputing the pointer at the call site cannot
/// have that failure.
///
/// And the wrapper that would genuinely help is not a wrapper. What every language that
/// ships a `CString` also ships — and documents as the default — is a *scoped* form,
/// `withCString`/`with_cstring`, where the pointer cannot outlive the callback. That is
/// worth adding here later; a name around a `[]u8` is not a step toward it.
///
/// The buffer is the caller's to keep alive, and nothing in the language enforces that:
/// `let escapes = (xs: []u8) -> ^u8 => &xs[0]` compiles. Bind it, use it, and let it fall
/// out of scope after the call.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `self` contains a NUL byte, because C cannot represent one: the string would
/// arrive truncated at that byte, which is the silently-wrong answer this language traps
/// for everywhere else. Rust returns an error here instead; a trap is the same call
/// `split("")` makes, an argument with no meaningful answer rather than input a program
/// did not choose.
pub let cstring = pure (self: string) -> []u8 => {
  var bytes = self.encode_utf8()
  for i in 0..<bytes.len() {
    if bytes[i] == 0 { panic("std.ffi: a C string cannot contain a NUL byte") }
  }
  bytes.push(0)
  bytes
}

